steps.body:
A 2D array is an array of rows. Write it with nested braces, one inner pair per row:

```java
int[][] grid = {
    {1, 2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6}
};
```

`grid[r][c]` reads row `r`, column `c`, both zero-based, so `grid[1][0]` is `4`. `grid.length` is the number of rows and `grid[r].length` is the number of columns in row `r`. To visit every cell, loop over the rows outside and the columns inside, and end each row with `println()`:

```java
for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {
        if (c > 0) {
            System.out.print(" ");
        }
        System.out.print(grid[r][c]);
    }
    System.out.println();
}
```

The `c > 0` check puts a space between values without leaving one at the end of the line.

Gotcha: the order is always row, then column. Writing `grid[c][r]` reads the wrong cell, or throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` when the grid is not square.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] grid = {
            {1, 2, 3},
            {4, 5, 6},
            {7, 8, 9}
        };

        System.out.println("Rows: " + grid.length + ", columns: " + grid[0].length);
        for (int r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {
                if (c > 0) {
                    System.out.print(" ");
                }
                System.out.print(grid[r][c]);
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
        System.out.println("Center: " + grid[1][1]);
    }
}

