import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] minutes = {
            {30, 45, 20},
            {60, 15},
            {40, 40, 25, 35}
        };

        int week = 1;
        for (int[] row : minutes) {
            int total = 0;
            for (int m : row) {
                total += m;
            }
            System.out.println("Week " + week + ": " + total + " minutes");
            week++;
        }

        int[][] copy = new int[minutes.length][];
        for (int r = 0; r < minutes.length; r++) {
            copy[r] = minutes[r].clone();
        }
        copy[1][0] = 50;

        System.out.println("Original: " + Arrays.deepToString(minutes));
        System.out.println("Copy: " + Arrays.deepToString(copy));
    }
}

steps.body:
When rows need to grow, use a list of lists: `List<List<Integer>>`. The outer list starts empty, so add one new inner list per row:

```java
List<List<Integer>> grid = new ArrayList<>();
for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {
    grid.add(new ArrayList<>());
}
grid.get(0).add(5);      // append to row 0
grid.get(2).add(7);      // row 2 grows on its own
int first = grid.get(0).get(0);   // row 0, column 0
```

`get(r)` returns row `r` as a `List<Integer>`, then `.get(c)` or `.add(x)` works on that row. Each row grows independently, and `println(grid)` prints `[[5], [], [7]]`.

Gotcha: create the inner list inside the loop. Adding one shared list several times gives you several references to the same row, so every add shows up in all of them:

```java
List<Integer> row = new ArrayList<>();
for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++) {
    grid.add(row); // wrong: all rows are one list
}
```

example.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<List<Integer>> groups = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            groups.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        for (int n = 1; n <= 10; n++) {
            groups.get(n % 2).add(n);
        }
        groups.get(1).add(11); // only the odds row grows

        System.out.println(groups);
        System.out.println("Evens (" + groups.get(0).size() + "): " + groups.get(0));
        System.out.println("Odds (" + groups.get(1).size() + "): " + groups.get(1));
        System.out.println("First even: " + groups.get(0).get(0));
    }
}

